  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nSprawling high pressure over our region is the primary driver of \n(no) weather heading into the new week. A highly amplified upper-\nlevel ridge axis extends along the Mississippi River this afternoon, \nwhile the axis of the surface high pressure system over the \nNortheast US extends along the Appalachians and over the Gulf Coast. \nThis subsident, dry weather regime with large diurnal temperature\nranges will continue through at least Thursday, and perhaps longer\ndepending on which longer range guidance verifies.\n\nHave extended the \"Elevated\" fire danger graphic accordingly as \nlow humidity and warm temperatures that have stressed and dried \nout vegetation transitioning out of the growing season. The ridge \nwill be strongest today and Monday, and then should gradually be \nsuppressed by the westerlies thereafter, but do not expect any\nsignificant change in the sensible weather at the surface, \nthrough at Thursday. \n\nBy next weekend, the general idea in the guidance is for at\nleast some moist return flow and possible interaction with a \nshortwave/cold front that could bring at least low to medium rain\nchances. There is a lot of divergence in the global model guidance\nat the end of the seven day forecast time frame, so would follow\nspecific forecast details with caution given the uncertainty and\nlow forecast confidence. Regardless of any rain chances, \ntemperatures for the upcoming Halloween day/night may push 10 \ndegrees warmer than normal. /NF/EC/",
